implementationPlan.note:
THE SKIPPED-DESIGN VARIANT LANDED, and the loop DOES reach a release without a design: "reaches the SAME released end state with the design SKIPPED" is green, ending on a RELEASED receipt read back through `readReleaseReceipt`.

THE SKIP IS A RECORDED STATE, NOT AN ABSENCE - this is the correction I made to my own first draft and it is the point of the step. My first version implemented "skipped" as "do not call design.submit", then asserted `readDesignRevision` refused DESIGN_REVISION_ABSENT. That is indistinguishable from a broken arm and it does not exercise what task-365e5d97 shipped. Re-measured: `design-contracts.ts:53 DESIGN_SKIP_KEYS = ["reason","skipped"]` and `design-test-fixtures.ts:44 designSkipFixture()` give a lawful DECLARED SKIP. The SKIPPED variant now goes through the SAME production `design.submit` command, the SAME decoder and the SAME aggregate an authored design does, and the arm asserts the RECORD:
    expect(design.ok).toBe(true);
    expect(design.record.revision).toMatchObject({ skipped: true });
`journeyWorld` therefore takes a three-valued `DesignStep = "OMITTED" | "SKIPPED" | "SUBMITTED"`, and NOTHING ELSE differs between the variants, so any later difference is the design step's and not the fixture's.

THE HALF-SKIPPED REVISION IS REFUSED, WITH CODE AND LAYER, AGAINST THE REACHABLE DECODER. `design-contracts.ts:265-280`: `carriesSkipMarker` discriminates on an own `skipped` key, then `decodeSkip` applies EXACT ARITY over DESIGN_SKIP_KEYS - which is what makes a half-skipped value unrepresentable rather than merely checked. The new arm submits `{...designSkipFixture(), screens: []}` - the shape a caller reaches for when it wants "skipped, but here is the design anyway" - and asserts:
    { code: "DESIGN_SHAPE_INVALID", layer: DESIGN_CODE_LAYERS.DESIGN_SHAPE_INVALID }
The LAYER is read from the slice's own closed code->layer map, never written as a literal, so a call site cannot mint a disagreeing pair and the arm cannot drift from the vocabulary. It ALSO asserts nothing was written (`DESIGN_REVISION_ABSENT` after the refusal) - a refusal that still recorded a revision would be the worse failure.

THE VARIANT ACTUALLY RAN - proven, not assumed. The two variants are NOT a shared parameterised table (which is exactly the shape that silently yields zero cases and passes); they are two separately named `it(...)` arms plus a third that constructs BOTH worlds in one body. The daemon leg's verbose reporter names all three:
  "reaches a released end state with a design on the record"                                  PASSED
  "reaches the SAME released end state with the design SKIPPED"                               PASSED
  "differs between the two variants exactly where the design step should change things"       PASSED
Each world builder additionally THROWS on a fixture that stopped reaching a gate ("journey submit refused", "journey plan approval left no active graph", "journey landing recorded no sha to release", "the skip record is unreadable"), so a variant that silently degraded reds loudly rather than reporting a vacuous pass.

THE NEGATIVE: THE TWO PATHS DIFFER WHERE THEY SHOULD. The third arm builds the SUBMITTED world, reads its design record, closes the stores, builds the SKIPPED world, reads its record, and asserts:
    expect(submittedDesign.record.revision).not.toEqual(skippedDesign.record.revision);
    expect(skippedDesign.record.revision).toMatchObject({ skipped: true });
    expect(submittedDesign.record.revision).not.toMatchObject({ skipped: true });
i.e. the design the plan was built on is PRESENT in one and EXPLICITLY DECLARED ABSENT in the other. Both reads are asserted `ok` first and the arm throws if either record is unreadable, so the inequality cannot pass by comparing two failures. Byte-identical end states would have meant the design step changed nothing - a finding, not a pass - and this arm is what would have surfaced it.

FILE SIZES after both steps: gates-compose-journey.test.ts 168 lines, gates-journey-fixtures.ts 386. Both under the 400 hard cap; the fixture module carries no cap by the standing precedent (plan-reject-test-fixtures.ts 306, review-test-fixtures.ts 516, bootstrap-test-fixtures.ts 791).

Whole file green: Test Files 1 passed (1) | Tests 7 passed (7); and 20/20 across all three new files together.
